Each folder is a chapter of the manual, each page in it one topic of that
chapter. A new topic gets its own page in the matching chapter; a new chapter
gets a new folder.

## Developer documentation

`docs/content/5.dev/` holds the contributor documentation: build, development
setup, architecture, policies, file formats, debugging. It is written for
people who build or hack on XCSoar, never for pilots. Update it when the build,
the tooling, an internal format or a development process changes; the user
manual stays untouched by that. Which page covers which topic, and which of
them to read before a change, is listed in `.cursor/rules/developer-docs.mdc`.

## Files and frontmatter

- The number prefix of a folder or file only sets the order; the rest of the
  name is the URL: `1.manual/04.navigation/05.waypoints.md` is
  `/manual/navigation/waypoints`.
- Within one folder, all prefixes have the same number of digits, as many as
  the largest number needs, with leading zeros (`00.preface.md` to
  `15.license.md`, `000.height-gps.md` to `134.previous-waypoint.md`). The
  order is sorted as text, and `10` would come before `2` otherwise. Renumber
  the folder when the largest number gains a digit.
- A folder starts with its first page. Only when a chapter needs an
  introduction of its own does it get a first page named after that content
  (`00.concepts.md`, `00.task-modes.md`), never `index` or `overview`.
- Every page starts with a YAML frontmatter with `title` and `description`.
  The title is the heading of the page, the browser tab and the print. The
  description is the meta description and the text on cards, at most 220
  characters. Put the essential information into its first 82 characters:
  the previous/next links below every page show only that much of it. Quote
  it when it contains a colon, otherwise `nuxt generate` fails.
- A page has up to three names, each as short as its place allows:
  - `title`: the full name, at most 40 characters, without quotes
    (`Status tabs Flight and Time`, not `Status tabs ‘Flight’ and ‘Time’`).
  - `navigation.title`: the name in the sidebar, at most 22 characters, the
    width the sidebar shows. Set it when the title is longer, or when a
    shorter name says the same (`Abort and alternates` for `Abort/resume the
    task and Alternates`, `Analysis` for `Analysis dialogue`). A folder sets
    it as `title` in its `.navigation.yaml` (`Atmosphere`), which is also the
    chapter heading in the print.
  - The file or folder name: the navigation title as a slug, at most 25
    characters, without filler words (`11.abort-and-alternates.md`,
    `05.tasks/`). The whole URL stays under 60 characters.
- `permalink: <id>` in the frontmatter gives a page a short, stable URL
  `/go/<id>` for links from the XCSoar app (lower-case letters, digits and
  hyphens; unique, the build fails otherwise). Only pages the app links to
  get one, and only when the link is needed; never rename or remove an id
  the app uses. The app appends an anchor itself: `/go/map-display#conf-...`.
- No `# H1` in the text; the title comes from the frontmatter. Headings start
  at `##`. No numbering in headings. Three empty lines before every `##`
  heading, two before `###`, one before `####` and below, and one after the
  frontmatter when the page starts with a heading.

### InfoBox pages

`docs/content/3.infobox/*.md` are two parts:

- The **frontmatter** (`title`, `infobox.*`) is generated from
  `src/InfoBoxes/Content/Factory.cpp` and `Type.hpp`. Never edit it by hand.
  After any change to an InfoBox in the code, run
  `node docs/scripts/import-infoboxes.mjs` and commit the result.
  `infobox.help` is the help text of the app and the meta description of the
  page. `infobox.comment` is the comment next to the enumerator in
  `src/InfoBoxes/Content/Type.hpp`; it is kept although the pages do not show
  it yet.
- The **markdown body** is written by hand and kept by the script. A new
  InfoBox gets a body that goes beyond the help text: how the value is
  computed, what it needs (polar, wind, task, connected device), when it is
  useful, what its colours mean when the value is drawn in colour, and what
  happens when it is tapped or long-pressed when the InfoBox reacts to that.
  Never repeat the help text; the script warns when the body does.

## Text conventions

- Hard wrap at 80 columns, filling each line. Exceptions: table rows, lines
  with a link or URL that cannot be broken, and the frontmatter.
- One space between sentences, never two. A hard line break is a backslash at
  the end of the line, not two spaces.
- Every file ends with a newline, markdown, YAML and JSON alike.
- Typographic apostrophe ’. Unicode instead of LaTeX maths and symbols: `°`,
  `×`, `γ`, `≥`. Hyphen-minus as in the sources, no dashes.
- Open questions as `<!-- TODO ... -->` at the end of a line, never on a line
  of its own inside a paragraph: a comment line splits the paragraph in the
  rendered page.

### Markup with one meaning each

| Markup | Meaning | Examples |
|--------|---------|----------|
| `` `Backticks` `` | Shown on screen, or typed: buttons, menu items, dialogue and page names, settings, option values, messages, gesture IDs, file names, paths, commands, code, build targets | `Task`, `Arrival height`, `Site Files / Site Files`, `On`, `REPLAY`, `DR`, `XCSoarData`, `make TARGET=UNIX` |
| `*italic*` | A term where it is introduced, jargon, emphasis | *gestures*, *snail trail*, *dolphin* style, *by how much* |
| `**bold**` | Leads of list items, highlighted letters | **Task**: ..., **T**ask manager |
| `“...”` | A real quotation or a word used loosely | “as is”, “armed”, a paper title |
| nothing | Product, company and example names, abbreviations | SkyLines, Vega, Bern TMA, (AGL) |

- Menu paths: `Nav 1/2` → `Task`, with the arrow →.
- Never ‘single quotes’, and no mixed forms like ‘*Circling zoom*’.

### Links

- Internal links are absolute paths: `/manual/navigation/waypoints`,
  `/manual/configuration/map-display#conf-waypointicons`,
  `/infobox/thermal-30s`. Never relative paths or `.md`.
- The link text is the navigation title of the target, or its title when it
  has none. Do not repeat the title after the link.
- Settings link to `#conf-...` anchors declared on the field that describes
  them: `:::field{name="Landable symbols" #conf-waypointicons}`. Anchors need
  to be unique within a page only.
- External URLs as `<https://...>` or `[text](https://...)`.

## Components

Each section is also printed to a PDF (`npm run pdf -- manual`). The print
view is rendered without JavaScript and paginated by Paged.js, so there is no
interaction in the PDF: nothing can be opened, hovered, scrolled or played.
The components below render correctly on the web and in the PDF.

MDC syntax: a block component is written as `::name` on a line of its own,
optionally with `{attributes}`, its content on the following lines, and `::`
on a line of its own to close it. The number of colons is the nesting depth:
a component inside another block component uses one colon more, opening and
closing with the same number. `::field-group` contains `:::field` blocks,
which are closed with `:::`; a component inside a `:::field` would use four.
An inline component is `:name{attributes}` inside a sentence.

| Syntax | Use for |
|--------|---------|
| `::field-group` with `:::field{name="..."}` | Description lists (settings, buttons, dialogue fields), not data tables |
| `::tip`, `::note`, `::warning` | Hints and warnings set apart from the text |
| `::gesture-aside{id="DR"}` ... `::` | A paragraph with the gesture drawing in a column beside it; `id` takes one or several gesture IDs separated by spaces |
| `:gesture{id="U" size="sm"}` | A gesture drawing inside a sentence |
| `:gesture{id="DU" float}` | A gesture drawing floated at the start of a paragraph inside a `:::field`, the text wraps around it |
| `![Alt](/img/figures/x.png "Caption")` | A figure with caption; `{width=32}` for icons; never scaled above natural size |
| GFM tables | Data; they take the width of their content, no HTML, no colspan |
| `::card`, `::u-page-*` | Cards and page sections, as on the landing page |

Gesture IDs are those of `Data/Input/default.xci`. Images are referenced by
their URL (`/img/figures/`, `/img/drawings/`, `/img/icons/`); where the files
live is configured in `nuxt.config.ts` and described in `docs/README.md`.
Prefer SVG for icons.

Wide tables (seven or more columns of icons or check marks) get sideways
headings in print; tables with more rows than a page break across pages. Menu
tables use `| Menu page | Key 6 | Key 7 | Key 8 | Key 9 | Key 0 |`.

Prefer these components. Other components of Nuxt UI or Docus, or an own Vue
component, are fine where they make a page clearly better; then give the PDF
something to show, such as a link or a still image next to a video, and check
the print output.

## Screenshots

- Take them from the UNIX build at its default size:
  `xcsoar -640x480 -touchscreen` for landscape, `xcsoar -portrait -touchscreen`
  for portrait, dialogues cropped without window frame. PNG at 1:1, no HiDPI,
  no device frame. This matches the existing figures and stays sharp in the
  PDF, where figures are at most 110 mm wide.
- Use the same demo profile, map and task for all figures.
- Name them `<topic>_<detail>.png` in the figures directory and replace an
  existing figure of the same view instead of adding a variant.

## Check before a patch

```bash
cd docs
npm run build:docs
```

This is what the workflow runs, and it has to succeed. It writes the pages
and the PDFs to `.output/public`. Read the pages you changed there, and the
PDF of the section you changed when the change shows in print, such as a
table, a figure or a component. `npm run generate` builds the pages alone,
and `npm run pdf -- <section>` makes the PDF of one of them, `manual`,
`quick-guide` or `dev`, from the pages already generated.

The "Last updated" line below a page comes from git; it does not change after
`git apply`, only after a commit. Never regenerate
`docs/content-history.json`.
